  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAn omega blocking pattern is spread across North America early \nthis morning, with the CONUS ridge axis extending from eastern \nMontana through New Mexico. The ridge axis is forecast to slowly\ndrift eastward through this weekend while being undercut by a \nshortwave traversing the southern US. The Northern Plains will \nremain under a south-southeast low-level/surface flow pattern \nthrough Saturday night, with breezy conditions at times. The \nstagnant pattern could maintain stubborn low clouds and \novernight/morning fog into Saturday, although winds may be too \nstrong for fog tonight into Saturday morning. That is not the \ncase this morning though, as a Dense Fog Advisory remains in \neffect for Slope and Bowman Counties. Guidance continues to \nfavor dense fog across most of western North Dakota this \nmorning, but so far it has remained more low stratus than fog \noutside of the far southwest. The clouds pose a challenging \ntemperature forecast. Early morning temperatures are in the \nupper teens to mid 20s to the east of the clouds and lower to \nmid 30s underneath the clouds. The high temperature forecast \nfor this afternoon was trended cooler than the NBM 50th \npercentile in western North Dakota, where confidence is higher \nin low clouds persisting through the day. It may still be \nwarmer there than farther east though, with forecast highs \ntoday ranging from the upper 30s east to mid 40s far west. \nBreezy conditions and low clouds could keep overnight \ntemperatures warmer than the deterministic NBM values of lower \n20s to lower 30s, but these are already warmer than the 50th \npercentile, so will refrain from deviating at this time. On \nSaturday, there is a higher probability that low clouds will \nclear as south- southeast surface winds increase to 20-30 mph, \nwith gusts around 30-40 mph. This should allow warmer high \ntemperatures in the lower 40s east to lower and mid 50s west. \nOn Sunday, winds should begin to relax as the surface pressure \ngradient weakens. A mostly sunny sky is favored on Sunday with \nhighs around 50 to 60.\n\nFrom early to mid next week, a broader upper level ridge is \nforecast to emerge over the Plains and slide eastward through \nthe Mississippi River Valley. This will transition flow aloft \nover the Northern Plains to southwesterly ahead of a progressive\nshortwave moving from southern California early Tuesday toward \nthe Northern Plains on Wednesday. The forecast remains dry \nthrough Tuesday, with well above normal high temperatures in the\nmid 50s to lower 60s on Monday and lower to mid 50s on Tuesday.\nEnsemble confidence is high in the broad timing and trajectory \nof the shortwave, but cluster analysis reveals uncertainty in \nwave amplitude and its evolution from Thursday onward as a \ndeeper trough digs into the western CONUS. All clusters bring \nprecipitation across the region Wednesday into Thursday, \nalthough a GEFS-heavy cluster has notably lighter QPF than the \nother 3. Temperatures may be cold enough for snow to mix in \nWednesday night into Thursday, but the probability of \nmeasurable snow remains low and is limited to western North \nDakota. The active pattern may continue into the following \nweekend, but ensembles show high spread in the evolution of the\nmid to late week western CONUS trough. A distinct cooling trend\nis advertised by the NBM from mid to late next week, with \ntemperatures favored to return closer to average.",
